# Selecto Domain Extensions Workbook

## Setup 1) Install Dependencies

Run this cell first. It installs the core dependencies and loads the two
plain-map domain fixtures. No database or Ecto Repo is started.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_core!()
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../lib/selecto_livebooks/domains/customer_domain.ex", __DIR__))
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../lib/selecto_livebooks/domains/order_domain.ex", __DIR__))
```

## Overview

This workbook focuses on domain-level extension surfaces that sit just above
ordinary query composition:

1. View-backed domain metadata with `source_kind` and `readonly`
2. Published view specs with `Selecto.ViewPublisher.build_sql/2`
3. Overlay-based domain extension with `Selecto.Config.OverlayDSL`

These examples are SQL-contract oriented, so they intentionally use
an explicit compile-only PostgreSQL runtime context when no live database execution is required.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  domain[domain config]
  meta[source metadata]
  publish[published view spec]
  overlay[overlay merge]
  sql[compiled SQL / DDL]

  domain --> meta --> sql
  domain --> publish --> sql
  domain --> overlay --> sql
```

## Setup 2) Imports and Helpers

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros
import Selecto.Sigil

alias Selecto.Config.Overlay
alias Selecto.ViewPublisher
alias SelectoLivebooks.Domains.{CustomerDomain, OrderDomain}

defmodule DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers do
  @moduledoc false

  def explain(label, query), do: Selecto.Livebook.explain(label, query)

  def show_published_view(label, domain, view_key) do
    spec = domain.published_views[view_key]

    case ViewPublisher.build_sql(domain, spec) do
      {:ok, result} ->
        IO.puts("\n=== #{label} ===")
        IO.puts("Kind: #{inspect(result.kind)}")
        IO.puts("Database name: #{result.database_name}")
        IO.puts("\nDDL:\n#{result.ddl}")

        if result.index_statements != [] do
          IO.puts("\nIndex statements:")
          Enum.each(result.index_statements, &IO.puts("  " <> &1))
        end

        {:ok, result}

      {:error, errors} ->
        raise "Published view compilation failed: #{inspect(errors)}"
    end
  end
end
```

## 1) View-Backed Domain Metadata

Use `source_kind` and `readonly` when the domain points at a database view or
materialized view instead of a base table.

```elixir
active_customer_view_domain = %{
  name: "Active Customers View",
  source: %{
    source_table: "active_customers_view",
    primary_key: :customer_id,
    source_kind: :view,
    readonly: true,
    fields: [:customer_id, :name, :tier],
    redact_fields: [],
    columns: %{
      customer_id: %{type: :integer},
      name: %{type: :string},
      tier: %{type: :string}
    },
    associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{},
  joins: %{},
  default_selected: ["customer_id", "name", "tier"],
  default_order_by: [{"name", :asc}]
}

customer_tier = "premium"

view_query =
  Selecto.configure(active_customer_view_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), validate: false)
  |> Selecto.select(select([customer_id, name, tier]))
  |> Selecto.filter(~SELECTO"tier == ^customer_tier")
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([asc(name)]))

IO.inspect(Map.take(active_customer_view_domain.source, [:source_kind, :readonly]), label: "View metadata")
DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.explain("View-backed domain query", view_query)
```

## 2) Published Views and Materialized Views

`published_views` lets a domain register stable SQL publication contracts for
generated views and materialized views.

The spec explicitly chooses the adapter. This cell only compiles DDL; it does
not create or refresh database objects. Ordinary views cannot have indexes.
Concurrent refresh of a populated materialized view requires an appropriate
unique, non-partial index; the example supplies one on `order_id`.

```elixir
published_order_domain =
  OrderDomain.domain()
  |> Map.put(:published_views, %{
    order_rollup: %{
      adapter: SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter,
      database_name: "reporting.order_rollup",
      kind: :view,
      query: fn selecto ->
        selecto
        |> Selecto.select(select([as(id, "order_id"), as(status, "status"), as(inserted_at, "inserted_at")]))
      end,
      columns: %{
        order_id: %{type: :integer},
        status: %{type: :string},
        inserted_at: %{type: :utc_datetime}
      },
      indexes: []
    },
    recent_order_snapshot: %{
      adapter: SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter,
      database_name: "reporting.recent_order_snapshot",
      kind: :materialized_view,
      query: fn selecto ->
        selecto
        |> Selecto.select(select([as(id, "order_id"), as(status, "status"), as(inserted_at, "inserted_at")]))
      end,
      columns: %{
        order_id: %{type: :integer},
        status: %{type: :string},
        inserted_at: %{type: :utc_datetime}
      },
      indexes: [
        %{columns: [:order_id], unique: true},
        %{columns: [:inserted_at], concurrently: true}
      ]
    }
  })

DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.show_published_view(
  "Published SQL view",
  published_order_domain,
  :order_rollup
)

DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.show_published_view(
  "Published materialized view",
  published_order_domain,
  :recent_order_snapshot
)

IO.puts("\nRefresh SQL:")
IO.puts(ViewPublisher.refresh_sql(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, "reporting.recent_order_snapshot", concurrently: true))
```

## 3) Extend a Base Domain with Overlay DSL

Use overlays when you want to keep the base domain stable but layer in extra
schemas, associations, joins, filters, or columns.

```elixir
defmodule DomainExtensionsLab.CustomerOrdersOverlay do
  use Selecto.Config.OverlayDSL

  defschema(:orders, %{
    source_table: "orders",
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :order_number, :status, :total, :customer_id, :inserted_at],
    redact_fields: [],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer},
      order_number: %{type: :string},
      status: %{type: :string},
      total: %{type: :decimal},
      customer_id: %{type: :integer},
      inserted_at: %{type: :utc_datetime}
    },
    associations: %{}
  })

  defsource_assoc(:orders, %{
    queryable: :orders,
    field: :orders,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :customer_id
  })

  defjoin(:orders, %{
    name: "Orders",
    type: :left,
    source: "orders",
    on: [%{left: "id", right: "customer_id"}],
    fields: %{
      order_number: %{type: :string},
      status: %{type: :string},
      total: %{type: :decimal}
    }
  })
end

overlay_customer_domain =
  CustomerDomain.domain()
  |> Overlay.merge(DomainExtensionsLab.CustomerOrdersOverlay.overlay())

overlay_query =
  Selecto.configure(overlay_customer_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), validate: false)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, tier, orders.order_number, orders.status, orders.total]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([asc(name), desc(orders.total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

IO.inspect(Map.keys(overlay_customer_domain.joins), label: "Merged joins")
DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.explain("Overlay-extended customer domain", overlay_query)
```

## 5) Generated View Domain Replacement

When a published view becomes the stable contract, swap the handwritten base
domain for the generated view domain and keep overlays focused on local
extensions.

```elixir
generated_view_domain =
  CustomerDomain.domain()
  |> put_in([:name], "customer_order_summary_view")
  |> put_in([:source, :source_table], "customer_order_summary_view")
  |> put_in([:source, :fields], [:id, :name, :tier, :order_count, :gross_total])
  |> put_in([:source, :columns], %{
    id: %{type: :integer},
    name: %{type: :string},
    tier: %{type: :string},
    order_count: %{type: :integer},
    gross_total: %{type: :decimal}
  })

generated_view_query =
  Selecto.configure(generated_view_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), validate: false)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, tier, order_count, gross_total]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(gross_total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.explain("Generated view-backed domain replacement", generated_view_query)
```

## 6) Overlay Filters and Columns

Overlays can add derived columns and reusable filter metadata alongside join
extensions without mutating the base domain module.

```elixir
overlay_with_reporting_metadata =
  overlay_customer_domain
  |> Overlay.merge(%{
    filters: %{
      active_tier: %{
        label: "Active customer tier",
        field: "tier",
        operators: [:eq, :in]
      }
    },
    columns: %{
      tier_label: %{type: :string, label: "Tier Label"}
    }
  })

overlay_filter_query =
  Selecto.configure(overlay_with_reporting_metadata, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), validate: false)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, tier, orders.order_number, orders.total]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(tier == "gold"))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(orders.total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

IO.inspect(Map.keys(Map.get(overlay_with_reporting_metadata, :filters, %{})), label: "Overlay filters")
DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.explain("Overlay-defined filter metadata", overlay_filter_query)
```

## 7) Published View Feeding a Downstream Domain

A view-backed domain can be treated as a compact reporting source for another
notebook or app.

```elixir
downstream_view_domain =
  generated_view_domain
  |> put_in([:default_selected], ["name", "tier", "order_count", "gross_total"])
  |> put_in([:default_order_by], [{"gross_total", :desc}])

downstream_view_query =
  Selecto.configure(downstream_view_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), validate: false)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, tier, order_count, gross_total]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(order_count >= 2))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(gross_total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.explain("Downstream view-backed domain", downstream_view_query)
```

## 8) Checked-In Generated-Domain Fixture

For examples that need a stable view-backed domain contract, keep a tiny fixture
module in the repo instead of rebuilding the map in every notebook cell.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/domain_extension_fixture.exs", __DIR__))

fixture_domain = SelectoLivebooks.DomainExtensionFixture.customer_order_summary_domain()

fixture_query =
  Selecto.configure(fixture_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), validate: false)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, tier, order_count, gross_total]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(gross_total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(5)

IO.inspect(fixture_domain.source.source_kind, label: "Fixture source kind")
IO.inspect(fixture_domain.source.readonly, label: "Fixture readonly")
DomainExtensionsLab.Helpers.explain("Checked-in view-domain fixture", fixture_query)
```

## 9) Overlay Conflict Resolution

When two overlays define the same join id, the later merge supplies the active
join definition. This cell makes the conflict visible before any query uses it.

```elixir
base_orders_join = overlay_customer_domain.joins.orders

conflicting_orders_overlay = %{
  joins: %{
    orders: %{
      base_orders_join
      | name: "Orders (inner conflict demo)",
        type: :inner
    }
  }
}

conflict_resolved_domain =
  overlay_customer_domain
  |> Overlay.merge(conflicting_orders_overlay)

%{
  before: %{name: base_orders_join.name, type: base_orders_join.type},
  after: %{
    name: conflict_resolved_domain.joins.orders.name,
    type: conflict_resolved_domain.joins.orders.type
  }
}
```

## 10) Published-View Column Drift Checklist

Compare the expected published-view columns with the checked-in fixture domain
before using the view as a long-lived downstream contract.

```elixir
expected_columns = MapSet.new(["id", "name", "tier", "order_count", "gross_total"])

fixture_columns =
  fixture_domain.source.columns
  |> Map.keys()
  |> Enum.map(&to_string/1)
  |> MapSet.new()

%{
  missing_from_fixture: expected_columns |> MapSet.difference(fixture_columns) |> MapSet.to_list(),
  extra_in_fixture: fixture_columns |> MapSet.difference(expected_columns) |> MapSet.to_list(),
  stable_contract?: MapSet.equal?(expected_columns, fixture_columns)
}
```

## 11) Generate Fixture Shape from Published View Spec

Derive the fixture contract from the published view columns so the handwritten
fixture can be checked against its source.

```elixir
published_spec = published_order_domain.published_views.order_rollup

generated_fixture_contract =
  published_spec.columns
  |> Enum.map(fn {name, config} -> {to_string(name), config.type} end)
  |> Map.new()

%{
  generated_columns: generated_fixture_contract,
  checked_in_fixture_columns: fixture_domain.source.columns |> Map.keys() |> Enum.map(&to_string/1)
}
```

## 12) Filter and Column Overlay Conflicts

The same merge-order rule applies to filters and derived columns.

```elixir
conflict_base =
  Overlay.merge(overlay_customer_domain, %{
    filters: %{tier_filter: %{label: "Tier v1", field: "tier"}},
    columns: %{tier_label: %{type: :string, label: "Tier Label v1"}}
  })

conflict_after =
  Overlay.merge(conflict_base, %{
    filters: %{tier_filter: %{label: "Tier v2", field: "tier"}},
    columns: %{tier_label: %{type: :string, label: "Tier Label v2"}}
  })

%{
  filter_label: conflict_after.filters.tier_filter.label,
  column_label: conflict_after.source.columns.tier_label.label
}
```

## 13) CI-Friendly View Drift Check

Package the drift check as a function that can be reused by a Mix task or CI
script.

```elixir
view_drift_check = fn expected, actual ->
  expected_set = MapSet.new(expected)
  actual_set = MapSet.new(actual)

  %{
    ok?: MapSet.equal?(expected_set, actual_set),
    missing: expected_set |> MapSet.difference(actual_set) |> MapSet.to_list(),
    extra: actual_set |> MapSet.difference(expected_set) |> MapSet.to_list()
  }
end

view_drift_check.(
  ["id", "name", "tier", "order_count", "gross_total"],
  fixture_domain.source.columns |> Map.keys() |> Enum.map(&to_string/1)
)
```

